ZAICODE

MISSION
Repair the audit/source blind spot first, then finish and independently verify T-24 without weakening existing ZAICODE product invariants.

CURRENT MACHINE TRUTH
- Active Work: T-24.
- Current phase: BUILD.
- Last known event: E-342.
- Do not treat the E-342 BUILD checkpoint as verification.
- Current audit representation does not contain the nested zcode repository even though the application implementation lives there.
- The workspace root git repository intentionally ignores /zcode/ to keep SAIPEN source identity cheap.
- Preserve that SAIPEN performance optimization. Do not solve the audit problem by removing /zcode/ from the root .gitignore.
- Production ZCode must remain untouched.

PRIMARY DEFECTS

A. AUDIT REPRESENTATION IS SOURCE-BLIND
The current AUDAPACK archive uses the workspace-root git inventory.
The application source lives in /zcode/, which is a separate nested git repository ignored by the root repository.
As a result the audit archive contains no zcode source files and cannot independently verify T-24 implementation claims.

Required:
1. Determine the correct AUDAPACK/project-side integration for nested repositories.
2. Preserve the fast root SAIPEN identity.
3. Make future ZAICODE audit archives carry enough nested-repository source truth to independently audit current changes.
4. At minimum include:
   - nested repository HEAD/branch/dirty status;
   - changed/untracked files relevant to the active Work;
   - source files touched by the active ticket;
   - required directly-coupled files/tests when needed for understanding and verification.
5. Prefer a generalized nested-repository solution over a ZAICODE-only hardcoded path if the AUDAPACK architecture supports it.
6. The archive manifest must state clearly whether a nested repository was included, partially represented, or unavailable.
7. Never report whole-project source completeness when the main nested implementation repository was not inventoried.

Acceptance:
- A newly produced ZAICODE audit archive exposes the actual T-24 implementation source or an explicit source-complete active-delta representation.
- An external auditor can inspect desktopPlatform, IPC/preload/main bridge, GLM provider helper/fallback, native branding/menu changes, SAIMAIL header/settings implementation, and associated tests from the archive.
- Root `saipen status` performance does not regress to the previous full-tree hashing behavior.

B. FINISH T-24
Re-read SRC-018 and E-341/E-342 before editing.

Verify and complete:
1. SAIMAIL launcher-preference bridge:
   - get launcher preferences mapped through renderer platform;
   - set autoRestartOnCrash works;
   - set saimailWorkspace works;
   - explicit init mailbox action works;
   - unconfigured state remains discoverable and actionable;
   - no mailbox content is decrypted/opened/marked-read by the ZAICODE header integration.
2. SAIMAIL titlebar:
   - visible even when unconfigured;
   - click when unconfigured opens the correct ZAICODE settings section;
   - configured state shows truthful unread/header state;
   - no duplicate old strip badge remains.
3. GLM:
   - official GLM account providers are recognized using real registry IDs, including account:zai-* and account:bigmodel-*;
   - legacy identifiers may remain compatible only where justified;
   - official GLM is not selected by default in ZAICODE;
   - quota/failure fallback uses the intended configured SAIRoute/SAIFREN route;
   - no silent fallback to an unrelated provider/model;
   - actual resolved provider/model remains observable separately from configured selection.
4. Branding/native surfaces:
   - tray says ZAICODE;
   - CUA indicator says ZAICODE;
   - deep-link dialogs say ZAICODE;
   - Explorer context menu uses ZAICODE's own registry key;
   - only release the upstream ZCode registry entry when it is demonstrably owned by this ZAICODE executable;
   - do not damage an installed production ZCode registration.
5. Rebuild packaged output and prove the running build contains the new source.

C. PACKAGED DATA ISOLATION
Create a separate ticket if necessary, but do not hide this finding:
- packaged ZAICODE currently shares ~/.zcode/cli/db/db.sqlite and artifacts with production ZCode;
- this contradicts the standalone mutable-state isolation contract.

Design a migration-safe repair:
1. Identify every CLI/session/artifact root used by packaged ZAICODE.
2. Define a ZAICODE-owned root.
3. Preserve existing ZAICODE sessions through explicit import/adoption/migration rather than continuing shared mutable ownership.
4. Never silently migrate or alter production ZCode data.
5. Add a regression test/probe proving production and ZAICODE roots are disjoint.

D. VERIFICATION INTEGRITY
Reconcile SRC-016 / T-22.
Do not leave the broad requirement "everything works as intended" VERIFIED solely from typecheck/lint/unit/build evidence when known product-contract gaps and unexecuted interactive flows remain.

Required:
- derive concrete uncovered requirements/findings;
- keep completed improvements closed;
- move unresolved product-contract items into explicit Work;
- coverage must distinguish source/unit/build proof from interactive runtime proof.

E. ROOT LAUNCHER HARDENING
Repair these independently reproducible defects:
1. tools/launcher/build.cmd must compile to a temporary/new executable first.
2. Do not rename/delete the current working launcher until compilation succeeds.
3. On compile failure, the previous ZAICODE.exe must remain launchable.
4. Reconcile documentation with the actual launcher chain.
5. ZAICODE.ps1 must either implement the same staged-build behavior as the canonical launcher or be explicitly documented as a reduced fallback.
6. Fix the malformed `tools\launcher\build.cmd` documentation string.
7. Replace fragile custom Windows command-line argument quoting with a correct implementation if arguments are supported.
8. Address or explicitly regenerate the absolute-path ZAICODE.lnk behavior so workspace relocation has truthful behavior.

VALIDATION GATES
Do not close T-24 from source inspection alone.

Run the repository's canonical pinned toolchain and record exact evidence for:
- focused ZAICODE UI tests;
- SAIMAIL model/header/settings tests;
- launcher-preference IPC/platform tests;
- provider-model selection tests using real account:zai-* / account:bigmodel-* IDs;
- GLM quota fallback tests;
- native naming/static checks for remaining user-visible ZCode branding;
- pnpm typecheck;
- pnpm lint with baseline-warning comparison;
- architecture check;
- ZAICODE bundle;
- packaged-source/artifact inspection;
- root launcher staged-build swap test;
- launcher compile-failure preservation test;
- production-ZCode registry non-interference test where safely automatable.

If interactive desktop behavior cannot be automated, do not convert that absence into a PASS.
Record the exact operator-only acceptance separately.

REGRESSION MATRIX
Protect:
- production ZCode installation and profile;
- root SAIPEN source-identity performance;
- ZAICODE account independence;
- zero-provider startup;
- SAIMAIL header-only trust boundary;
- queue/agent persistence;
- existing Direct routing;
- fail-closed unresolved router modes;
- launcher crash recovery;
- staged bundle replacement;
- local profile and settings persistence;
- Explorer integration ownership.

COLD RECOVERY
After every substantial milestone:
- checkpoint exact machine truth into SAIPEN;
- state files changed;
- commands/tests executed and results;
- active blockers;
- next exact action;
- source binding including nested zcode repository HEAD and dirty state.

Continue autonomously through all eligible work.
Do not stop merely because one milestone passed.
Stop only at a genuine capability/operator boundary or when no eligible work remains.
